From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:42:20 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A shaft is a shaft whatever the foliage rule says, and the
 dust is pushed for real

Patch 0039 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled).

The user's test in the Temple of Time, 2026-10-08: the Master Sword room's drawn shaft and its
floor patch were still there, and the tall window's light there read as "just ... a cutout in the
shadow, not actual additional light being added", the floor lit far less than the hall's while the
dust in the air above it showed the light. And on the dust: "link is passing right through the
particles rather than him interacting with them".

THE SHAFT. The foliage rule (patch 0017, the default) makes every translucent draw of more than two
triangles that reads depth solid. The pedestal shaft has four, the Deku sprout's six, a grotto's
twelve, so none was ever a candidate for 0029's beam test: no light of its own, never hidden by
0034, and as solid traced geometry the pedestal shaft stood in its window's light and blocked it,
which is the dim floor. Now every translucent draw is tested, and one found to be a shaft is a
see-through overlay no ray traces, drawn after the composite like a glow. In a place with windows,
while the local lights are traced, every shaft that leans in from above is drawn as nothing,
whether or not it was traced to a window in that frame; its direction still sets its window's
light. An upright shaft, a song's cylinder, is drawn as before.

THE PUSH. 0037 carried a mote gently and never past half a cell, so a figure walked through the
dust. Now a fresh trail sample's body sweeps the air clear (a mote within 32 units of its middle
is pushed out to that edge), and the carry, the spill and the lift are stronger, to 100 units in
all. The march looks each point of the ray up through the push, the cell the air there came from,
so a mote pushed in from a cell the ray never crosses is still found.

diff --git a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
--- a/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
+++ b/src/render/rt64_framebuffer_renderer.cpp
@@ -2628,12 +2628,26 @@ namespace RT64 {
                         // beamFromDraw). Only a draw this scene leaves out as see-through, that
                         // tests depth, with a fading texture: a shaft is never solid and never a
                         // cut-out. Rung 37 leaves every shaft as the game drew it.
-                        if (wasTranslucent && !solidDraw && !depthlessOverlay && otherMode.zCmp() && (cutoutRule != 37) &&
+                        //
+                        // A SHAFT IS A SHAFT WHATEVER THE FOLIAGE RULE SAYS (patch 0039). The
+                        // foliage rule (patch 0017, the default) makes every translucent draw of
+                        // more than two triangles that reads depth SOLID, so a shaft of four
+                        // triangles (the Temple of Time's pedestal beam), six (the Deku sprout's)
+                        // or twelve (a grotto's) was never a candidate here: no light of its own,
+                        // never hidden by 0034, and as solid traced geometry it stood in its own
+                        // window's light and blocked it, which is why that light read as only a
+                        // hole in the shadow (the user, 2026-10-08: "this window seems to just be a
+                        // cutout in the shadow, not actual additional light being added"). So the
+                        // test takes every translucent draw, and a draw it finds to be a shaft is a
+                        // see-through overlay no ray traces, drawn after the composite in its own
+                        // brightness, like a glow.
+                        if (wasTranslucent && !depthlessOverlay && otherMode.zCmp() && (cutoutRule != 37) &&
                             (call.callDesc.tileCount > 0) && (beamLights.size() < MaxBeamLights)) {
                             const DrawCallTile &beamTile = drawData.callTiles[call.callDesc.tileIndex];
                             interop::PointLight beam;
                             if (beamTile.valid && !beamTile.tileCopyUsed && (textureAlphaKind(beamTile.tmemHashOrID) == TextureAlphaKind::Fade) &&
                                 beamFromDraw(drawData, call, beam)) {
+                                raytracing.queryMask = TranslucentRayQueryMask;
                                 addBeam(beamLights, beam);
 
                                 // THE SHAFT GOES WHERE ITS WINDOW'S LIGHT STANDS IN FOR IT (patch
@@ -2647,8 +2661,17 @@ namespace RT64 {
                                 // A shaft falling any other way (a song's upright cylinder) is.
                                 const hlslpp::float3 falls = beam.direction;
                                 const bool lightsTraced = (rtResources->rtParams.level >= 2) && (rtResources->rtParams.tuneOcclusion.w > 0.0f);
-                                if (rtExternalEnvironment.windowLights && rtWindowSunKnown && lightsTraced &&
-                                    (float(hlslpp::dot(falls, rtWindowSunDirection)) > 0.995f)) {
+                                // EVERY SLANTED SHAFT, NOT ONLY THE ONE TRACED TO A WINDOW (patch
+                                // 0039, the user, 2026-10-08: "fully remove the light, you know, the
+                                // mimicked light from the Master Sword Room and instead replace it
+                                // with the same brightness and lighting behavior that the windows in
+                                // the main room are doing"). A shaft is only ever traced to a window
+                                // drawn in the same frame, and the tall window and its shaft are not
+                                // always both drawn, so the shaft stayed. In a place with windows a
+                                // shaft leaning in from above is the game's stand-in for a window's
+                                // light and goes; an upright one (a song's cylinder) stays.
+                                const bool upright = (-float(falls.y)) > 0.97f;
+                                if (rtExternalEnvironment.windowLights && lightsTraced && !upright) {
                                     hideShaft = true;
                                 }
                             }
